#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# lu

#% text
# `lu(m)` gives `[l, u, p]` with `l` unit lower triangular, `u` upper triangular and the rows of `m` in the order `p` equal to `dot(l, u)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The permutation comes from partial pivoting (for exact matrices the first nonzero pivot, for machine matrices the largest).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
lu([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [[[1, 0], [3, 1]], [[1, 2], [0, -2]], [1, 2]]

#% cell
lu([[0, 1], [1, 0]])
#% output
#= [[[1, 0], [0, 1]], [[1, 0], [0, 1]], [2, 1]]

#% cell
lu([[4.0, 3.0], [6.0, 3.0]])
#% output
#= [[[1.0, 0.0], [0.6666666666666666, 1.0]], [[6.0, 3.0], [0.0, 1.0]], [2, 1]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The factors give the permuted matrix back:

#% cell
m = [[2, 1, 1], [4, -6, 0], [-2, 7, 2]]; f = lu(m); dot(f[1], f[2]) == m[f[3]]
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The determinant is the product of the pivots, up to the sign of the permutation:

#% cell
[product(table(f[2][k, k], k, 3)), det(m)]
#% output
#= [-16, -16]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `qr`, `cholesky`, `det`, `linear_solve`
